\label{thm:polynomial-remainder}
Let  $U$ be a unitary, and let $f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x)=\operatorname{sf}(p_U(t))$ be the square-free part of its characteristic polynomial. Since \(U\) is diagonalizable, \(f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x)\) is the minimal polynomial of \(U\).

For \(k\geq 1\), define $\rho_k(x):=x^k\bmod f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x),$
where \(\rho_k(x)\) is the unique remainder satisfying $\deg \rho_k<\deg f_{\mathrm{sf}}=d_{\mathrm{sf}}$.
Then, for any \(\tau\in K\),
\begin{equation}
    U^k=\tau I \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad  \rho_k(x)=\tau,
\end{equation}
where the right-hand side means that \(\rho_k(x)\) is the constant polynomial \(\tau\).

To illustrate the method, consider the four-qubit (\(N=4\)) kicked Ising model defined above, with $J=h=g=b_1=b_N=\frac{\pi}{4}.$
For this parameter choice, a suitable number field is $K=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_8)$. The Hilbert-space dimension is $d=2^4=16$, and the characteristic polynomial of the Floquet unitary $U_F$ is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f_{U_F}(x) ={}&x^{16} +\zeta_8 x^{15} +\zeta_8 x^{11} +\zeta_4 x^{10} -\zeta_4 x^6 \\
&-\zeta_8^3 x^5 -\zeta_8^3 x +1,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\zeta_l =e^{2\pi i/l}$ denotes a primitive \(\ell\)th root of unity.
Differentiating \(f_{U_F}(x)\) gives
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f_{U_F}'(x) ={}&16x^{15} +15\zeta_8 x^{14} +11\zeta_8 x^{10} +10\zeta_4 x^9 \\
&-6\zeta_4 x^5 -5\zeta_8^3 x^4 -\zeta_8^3.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The greatest common divisor of $f_{U_F}(x)$ and $f_{U_F}'(x)$ is
\begin{equation}
\gcd\!\left(f_{U_F},f_{U_F}'\right) = x^6+\zeta_8 x^5+\zeta_8 x+\zeta_4.
\end{equation}
Therefore, the square-free part of the characteristic polynomial is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x) &= \frac{f_{U_F}(x)} {\gcd\!\left(f_{U_F}(x),f_{U_F}'(x)\right)} \\
&=x^{10}-\zeta_4,
\end{aligned}
\qquad
d_{\mathrm{sf}}=10.
\end{equation}
Here we can see that the original dimension of the system was $d=16$, but the dimension of square-free polynomial reduces to $d_{sf}=10$. Because \(U_F\) is unitary and hence diagonalizable, its minimal
polynomial contains each distinct eigenvalue exactly once. Since \(\zeta_4\in\mathcal{O}_K\), no denominator clearing is required as $c=1,$ implies $ f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x)=F_{\mathrm{sf}}(x).$
The polynomial discriminant is
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Disc}\!\left(F_{\mathrm{sf}}\right) = 10^{10}\zeta_4.
\end{equation}
For \(K=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_8)\), we have
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\left| N_{K/\mathbb{Q}} \!\left( \operatorname{Disc}\!\left(F_{\mathrm{sf}}\right) \right) \right|
&=10^{40}, \qquad \operatorname{Disc}(K/\mathbb{Q})=256,\\
N_{K/\mathbb{Q}}(c)&=1.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The resulting set of rational primes is therefore $\mathcal{P}=\{2,5\}.$
The degree bound entering the arithmetic criterion is $[K:\mathbb{Q}]\,d_{\mathrm{sf}}! = 4(10)! = 14\,515\,200.$ The maximal prime-power exponents compatible with the bound are $a_2=11,$ and $a_5=3,$ which give the universal exponent $E=2^{11}5^3=256\,000.$

We first determine whether an exact recurrence exists by evaluating the remainder at the universal exponent, $\rho_E(x) = x^E \bmod f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x).$
For the present example, \(E=256000\) and \(f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x)=x^{10}-\zeta_4\). Therefore,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\rho_E(x) &= x^{256000} \bmod \left(x^{10}-\zeta_4\right) \\
&= \left(x^{10}\right)^{25600} \bmod \left(x^{10}-\zeta_4\right) \\
&= \zeta_4^{25600} = 1.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Since \(\rho_E(x)\) is constant, \(U_F^E=I\), establishing that an exact recurrence exists.

Having established recurrence, we determine the minimal recurrence period by testing the divisors \(n\mid E\) and identifying the smallest
one for which $\rho_n(x) = x^n\bmod f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x)$ is constant. We obtain
\begin{equation}
\rho_{10}(x) = x^{10}\bmod\left(x^{10}-\zeta_4\right) = \zeta_4.
\end{equation}
and for every divisor \(n\mid E\) with \(1\leq n<10\), the remainder is nonconstant. Hence the minimal recurrence period is $n_{rec}=10$ with $U_F^{10}=\zeta_4 I=iI.$

For this parameter choice, \(U_F\) is a Clifford unitary. The arithmetic criterion itself, however, makes no assumption about Cliffordness and
therefore allows exact recurrence to be studied independently of the Clifford character of the Floquet circuit.

An element \(\alpha\in K\) is called an \emph{algebraic integer} if it satisfies a monic polynomial with integer coefficients. Equivalently, there exist \(a_0,\ldots,a_{r-1}\in\mathbb{Z}\) such that
\begin{equation}
    \alpha^r + a_{r-1}\alpha^{r-1} + \cdots + a_1 \alpha + a_0 = 0.
\end{equation}

The set of all algebraic integers contained in \(K\) forms a ring, called the \emph{ring of integers} of \(K\). It is denoted by
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{O}_K:=\left\{\alpha\in K:\alpha\text{ is an algebraic integer}\right\}.
\end{equation}

For \(K=\mathbb{Q}\), the only rational numbers that satisfy a monic polynomial with integer coefficients are the ordinary integers. Therefore,
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}=\mathbb{Z}.
\end{equation}

Consider
\begin{equation}
    K=\mathbb{Q}(i), \qquad i^2=-1.
\end{equation}
Since \(i\) satisfies the monic polynomial $x^2+1=0,$ it is an algebraic integer. The ring of integers of
\(\mathbb{Q}(i)\) is the ring of Gaussian integers,
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}(i)} =\mathbb{Z}[i]=\left\{a+bi:a,b\in\mathbb{Z}\right\}.
\end{equation}

Let  $ \zeta_n=e^{2\pi i/n}$ be a primitive \(n\)th root of unity, and consider the cyclotomic field $ K=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_n).$ Because \(\zeta_n\) satisfies the monic polynomial $x^n-1=0,$ it is an algebraic integer. In fact, the complete ring of integers of the cyclotomic field is $ \mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_n)}=\mathbb{Z}[\zeta_n] $  \cite[Theorem~6.4]{milneANT}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{Z}[\zeta_n]=\left\{ \sum_{j=0}^{\varphi(n)-1}a_j\zeta_n^j: a_j\in\mathbb{Z} \right\},
\end{equation}
where \(\varphi(n)\) is Euler's totient function. The Gaussian field is
the special cyclotomic case $\mathbb{Q}(i)=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_4).$

A nonempty subset \(I\subseteq A\) is called an \emph{ideal} of \(A\) if
\begin{equation}
    x,y\in I \quad\Longrightarrow\quad  x-y\in I,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    a\in A,\quad x\in I \quad\Longrightarrow\quad ax\in I.
\end{equation}
Thus, an ideal is closed under addition and subtraction and absorbs multiplication by arbitrary elements of the ring.

For an element \(\alpha\in A\), the \emph{principal ideal} generated by
\(\alpha\) is
\begin{equation}
    (\alpha):= \alpha A = \left\{\alpha a:a\in A \right\}.
\end{equation}
More generally, the ideal generated by
\(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_r\in A\) is
\begin{equation}
    (\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_r) := \left\{ a_1\alpha_1+\cdots+a_r\alpha_r: a_1,\ldots,a_r\in A \right\}.
\end{equation}

A proper ideal \(\mathfrak p\subsetneq A\) is called a \emph{prime ideal} if
\begin{equation}
    ab\in\mathfrak p  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad a\in\mathfrak p \quad\text{or}\quad b\in\mathfrak p
\end{equation}
for all \(a,b\in A\).

Every ideal of \(\mathbb{Z}\) is principal and has the form
\begin{equation}
    (n) = n\mathbb{Z} = \left\{ nk:k\in\mathbb{Z} \right\}
\end{equation}
for some nonnegative integer \(n\). If \(p\) is a rational prime, then
\begin{equation}
    (p)=p\mathbb{Z}
\end{equation}
is a prime ideal of \(\mathbb{Z}\). For example, \(5\mathbb{Z}\) is a
prime ideal, whereas \(6\mathbb{Z}\) is not, since
\[
2\cdot3\in6\mathbb{Z}, \qquad 2\notin6\mathbb{Z}, \qquad 3\notin6\mathbb{Z}.
\]

Let
\[
K=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_n),
\qquad
\mathcal{O}_K=\mathbb{Z}[\zeta_n].
\]
For a rational prime \(p\), the ideal generated by \(p\) in
\(\mathcal{O}_K\) is
\begin{equation}
    (p)
    =
    p\mathcal{O}_K.
\end{equation}
Since \(\mathcal{O}_K\) is a Dedekind domain, this ideal has a unique
factorization
\begin{equation}
    p\mathcal{O}_K
    =
    \mathfrak p_1^{\,e_1}
    \cdots
    \mathfrak p_g^{\,e_g}
\end{equation}
into distinct prime ideals of \(\mathcal{O}_K\).

As a concrete example, let \(\ell\) be a rational prime and consider
\[
K=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_\ell).
\]
The ideal
\begin{equation}
    \mathfrak l
    =
    (1-\zeta_\ell)
\end{equation}
is a prime ideal of \(\mathbb{Z}[\zeta_\ell]\), and
\begin{equation}
    (\ell)
    =
    \mathfrak l^{\,\ell-1}
    =
    (1-\zeta_\ell)^{\ell-1}
    \cite[Theorem~6.4]{milneANT}.
\end{equation}
Its contraction to \(\mathbb{Z}\) is
\begin{equation}
    \mathfrak l\cap\mathbb{Z}
    =
    (\ell).
\end{equation}
Therefore, \(\mathfrak l=(1-\zeta_\ell)\) is the unique prime ideal
lying above \(\ell\) in \(\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_\ell)\).

Let $f_{\mathrm{sf}}\in K[x]$ be monic and square-free, and let $L$ be its splitting field over $K$. Choose
$c\in\mathbb Z_{>0}$ such that
\begin{equation}
F_{\mathrm{sf}}(x)=c f_{\mathrm{sf}}(x) \in\mathcal O_K[x].
\end{equation}
The leading coefficient of $F_{\mathrm{sf}}$ is $c$ and its splitting field is also $L$.

We use the standard polynomial reduction criterion: if a polynomial in $\mathcal O_K[x]$ has leading coefficient not divisible by a prime ideal $\mathfrak p$, and its reduction modulo $\mathfrak p$ has no repeated roots, then $\mathfrak p$ is unramified in its splitting field \cite[Sec.~7.8, p.~180]{Keune2023NumberFields}. If $p\mid c$, the first alternative holds.

Suppose therefore that $p\nmid c$. Since $\mathfrak p$ lies above $p$, we have $\mathfrak p\cap\mathbb Z=(p)$. Thus $c\notin\mathfrak p$, so the leading coefficient of $F_{\mathrm{sf}}$ remains nonzero modulo $\mathfrak p$. In particular, reduction modulo $\mathfrak p$ preserves its degree.
Write
\begin{equation}
\Delta=\operatorname{Disc}(F_{\mathrm{sf}}) \in\mathcal O_K.
\end{equation}

Suppose that $\mathfrak p$ does not divide $(\Delta)$. Then $\Delta$ remains nonzero modulo $\mathfrak p$. Because the discriminant is a polynomial expression in the coefficients, reduction commutes with taking
the discriminant:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Disc}\!\left(\overline{F_{\mathrm{sf}}}\right) = \overline{\operatorname{Disc}(F_{\mathrm{sf}})} = \overline{\Delta} \neq 0.
\end{equation}
Here the bar denotes reduction modulo $\mathfrak p$. Consequently, $\overline{F_{\mathrm{sf}}}$ has no repeated roots in an algebraic closure of
$\mathcal O_K/\mathfrak p$.

The polynomial reduction criterion therefore implies that $\mathfrak p$ is unramified in $L/K$. This contradicts the hypothesis. Hence $\mathfrak p\mid(\Delta).$ Taking ideal norms preserves this divisibility. Since $\mathfrak p$ lies above $p$, its ideal norm is $p^f$ for some integer $f\geq1$. Therefore
\begin{equation}
p^f = \operatorname{Nm}(\mathfrak p) \mid \operatorname{Nm}((\Delta)) = \left|N_{K/\mathbb Q}(\Delta)\right|.
\end{equation}
It follows that
\begin{equation}
p\mid
\left|N_{K/\mathbb Q}
\bigl(\operatorname{Disc}(F_{\mathrm{sf}})\bigr)\right|.
\end{equation}
This proves the second alternative when $p\nmid c$,
and completes the proof.

Let $U$ be a unitary matrix whose entries lie in a number field $K$, and let $n$ be the smallest positive integer such that $U^n=\tau I$ for some scalar $\tau$. Let $L$ be the splitting field of $f_{\mathrm{sf}}$ over $K$. Using our previous work, \cite{anand2026arithmetic} we establish that $\mathbb Q(\zeta_n)\subseteq L.$ Now let $p$ be an odd prime dividing $n$. By Appendix~\ref{app:cyclotomic-discriminant},
$p$ ramifies in $\mathbb Q(\zeta_n)/\mathbb Q$. Since ramification indices multiply in the tower $\mathbb Q\subseteq\mathbb Q(\zeta_n)\subseteq L,$
the prime $p$ also ramifies in $L/\mathbb Q$.

Applying Lemma~\ref{lem:ramification of primes} to the tower $\mathbb Q\subseteq K\subseteq L$ gives two possibilities.
If $p$ ramifies in $K/\mathbb Q$, then
\begin{equation}
p\mid\operatorname{Disc}(K/\mathbb Q),
\end{equation}
by the field-discriminant criterion, see Appendix \ref{app:cyclotomic-discriminant}.


Otherwise, some prime ideal $\mathfrak p$ of $\mathcal O_K$ above $p$ ramifies in $L/K$. The ramification--discriminant lemma proved in
Appendix~\ref{app:ramification_discriminant_proof}
then gives
\begin{equation}
p\mid c
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
p\mid
\left|N_{K/\mathbb Q}
\bigl(\operatorname{Disc}(F_{\mathrm{sf}})\bigr)\right|.
\end{equation}
This proves the assertion for every odd prime divisor of $n$. Including $2$ separately completes the proof.
